CORE
main.cpp
--cpp20
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ concept satisfied: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ instantiated when odr-used: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
dropped the body
--
N5008 [expr.prim.req.general]/2, [dcl.type.decltype]/1,
[basic.def.odr]/5, [temp.inst]/4: `it++' in the requirement is an
unevaluated operand, so normal_iterator<opaque *>::operator++(int) is not
odr-used and its definition -- ill-formed for the incomplete opaque -- is
not instantiated.  The front end queued the body of every member named
while type-checking an expression, also in unevaluated operands, and
dropped this one ("dropped the body of function 'operator++'", bin53).
Requirements are now type-checked as unevaluated operands, and members
named there are not queued.  Reduced (cvise) from libstdc++'s
__normal_iterator<__pool_resource::_BigBlock *>
(cpp11_libstdcxx_header_drops/test_memory_resource.desc).  g++ 13 and
clang++ 18 accept this program, and it fails only its WRONG assertion.
